# Animation pattern: ghost window, never live widgets

Animating the real Tk window (SetWindowPos or `geometry` per frame, with
widgets re-laid-out) is jittery. Every window animation here uses the same
recipe — see `_ghost_morph`, `_collapse_live`, `_resize_glide` in
`timesheet/ui/overlay.py`, `winfx.Ghost`, and `timesheet/anim.py`.

1. **Snapshot** the current window (`winfx.capture`) and show it in a
   `winfx.Ghost(max_w, max_h, click_through=False)` exactly over the window.
   Wait one `DwmFlush` so it is on screen, then set the real window to
   `-alpha 0`.
2. **Animate only the ghost**: `anim.run(ms, frame, easing=...)` paced by
   `DwmFlush`; each frame is one `ghost.draw(rect, a, b, mix, opacity,
   border=...)`. The first ~20% dissolves the snapshot into a plain
   panel-coloured rectangle in place (`hold`), then the rectangle eases to the
   target rect. Never show a stretched snapshot of text. No Tk work, no
   `update()` per frame (except where input must stay live).
3. **Shield**: `_raise_shield(rect)` over the whole area touched (union of
   start and end rects) for the full animation. A fully transparent real
   window lets clicks fall through to the app behind and bring it forward.
   `Ghost(click_through=False)` windows refuse activation (WM_MOUSEACTIVATE
   -> MA_NOACTIVATE) so the foreground never moves.
4. **Do the real change once, hidden, after the animation**: freeze
   (`dwm.freeze`), set geometry / pack contents / rebuild the view, thaw,
   `update()`, `DwmFlush`, then restore `-alpha`.
5. **Reveal**: drop the shield, `ghost.set_click_through(True)`, fade the
   ghost out (~120 ms) over the live window, then close it.
6. Set `_morphing = True` for the duration (disables click-out collapse and
   re-entrancy), clean up ghosts/shield in `finally`, and keep a plain
   non-animated fallback if the ghost cannot be created.
7. Verify with real injected clicks over a second window behind the app
   (foreground must stay on the app) and by checking frame gaps are ~16.7 ms.
